          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "**E11 is the category for type 2 diabetes.** The ICD-10-CM passage (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11) says type 2 diabetes is E11 in Chapter 4, which covers endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases. Diabetes is split by cause, not by treatment. Type 2 is its own category, and an unspecified \"diabetes mellitus\" defaults to type 2. Taking insulin does not change the category.\n\n**The sources I searched did not explain E11.9.** The retrieved passages describe the E11 category but do not describe any five-character subcategory such as E11.9. I can't tell you from these sources what E11.9 means, when it applies, or how it differs from E11. Coding rules are also updated each year, so a code's current meaning should be checked against the current official ICD-10-CM tables, not this answer.\n\nIf you are coding a record, a trained medical coder or the official code book can confirm which code fits. If you are asking about your own records, the clinician or billing office that issued them can explain which code was used and why.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
